<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augustine of Canterbury</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 604</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Augustine of Canterbury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augustine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> was a monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> to convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>. Landing in Kent in 597, he baptised King Æthelberht and became the first archbishop of Canterbury, founding the cathedral and the monastery later named after him. His attempt to win over the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> failed, but his mission laid the foundations of the English Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury.jpg" alt="Augustine of Canterbury" width="230" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scriptorium de l'abbaye de Wearmouth-Jarrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LeningradBedeHiRes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
